<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kohala, Hawaii</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kohala is the northernmost district of the island of Hawaiʻi, formed by the old Kohala volcano. It is the traditional birthplace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i">Kamehameha I</a> and the site of Moʻokini <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heiau">Heiau</a>, one of the oldest temples in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>. Its dry leeward slopes supported the extensive Kohala field system of rain-fed farming, now an important archaeological landscape.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kohala-hawaii.jpg" alt="Kohala, Hawaii" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sn1per · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HawaiiIslandDistrict-NorthKohala.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The aliʻi nui stood at the head of a hierarchy of chiefs, priests and land managers enforced by the kapu system, and the island's dryland field systems in Kona and <b>Kohala</b> fed a large population.</blockquote><a href="/state/chiefdom-of-hawaii-island">Chiefdom of Hawaii Island</a></li></ul></section>
